        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A vapour barrier and a waterproofing membrane answer different questions. If you can see liquid water, the answer involves drainage and, where justified, an exterior membrane on the positive side of the wall. If the wall is merely damp and the room is humid with nothing visible, the answer is a vapour-retarding assembly on one face plus dehumidification.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The failure to avoid is sealing a stud cavity impermeably on both faces, which is what happens when an interior coating or poly on the masonry meets a second vapour barrier behind the drywall. Pick one face for vapour control, keep air-permeable insulation away from cold masonry, and run the three-day foil test before buying anything, because it tells you which of the two problems you actually have.
          </p>
        </div>

